## Access

- Students: no AdaLab account. The **course password** (`COURSE_PASSWORD`, rotated each
  semester at deploy time) is the access control, so `.adalab/app.json` sets
  `access_level: "public"`. Identity is self-registration by KUID — there are no per-student
  passwords.
- Admin: `ADMIN_PASSWORD`, compared constant-time. If it is unset the admin area is disabled
  (fail closed). Rotate by redeploying with a new value.
- Students stay signed in across a page refresh (and when they come back weeks later) via an
  opaque token in the URL — **no student passwords**. Only a hash of it is stored, it carries
  no personal data, it expires after `SESSION_TTL_DAYS`, and "Sign out" revokes it
  immediately. Note the URL is therefore a bearer credential: sharing the link shares the
  session, exactly as sharing a KUID already allows impersonation under the honour-system model.
- Environment variables (complete set): `DATA_DIR` (default `/asv-mnt/lab-data`),
  `COURSE_ID`, `COURSE_PASSWORD`, `ADMIN_PASSWORD`, `DEMO_MODE` (default `false`),
  `APP_PORT` (default 8000), `BASE_URL_PATH` (default the app slug — see URL prefix below),
  `SESSION_TTL_DAYS` (default 30), `LOG_PII` (default `false` — see Logging).

### Logging

Every meaningful action is recorded: student registrations (with timestamps), returning
students, group creation, **every submission**, **every correction** (with the old values, the
new values and which fields changed), answers saved, exports actually taken, all admin actions,
and all errors with their tracebacks.

It goes to three places, because each answers a different question:

- **The `event` table** — filterable by level / action / KUID in **Admin → Log**, downloadable
  as CSV. This is the teacher-facing record of what happened in a lab session.
- **stdout** — lands in the container / AdaLab log viewer, for whoever is on call.
- **`$DATA_DIR/<app>/events.jsonl`** — on the shared volume, so the log survives redeploys
  (stdout does not). Rotated at 5 MB to `events.jsonl.1`.

Personal data handling: the database and the volume log keep the KUID (same data processing
agreement as the results themselves); **stdout gets a pseudonymous `member:<id>` instead**,
because platform logs are aggregated more widely. Set `LOG_PII=true` to include KUIDs in
stdout too. Logging is best-effort by design — a failing log sink can never block a student's
submission.

### URL prefix (important)

Streamlit must know the sub-path it is served under, and it needs that prefix on incoming
requests. So this app sets **`stripped_prefix: false`** in `.adalab/app.json` and runs
Streamlit with `--server.baseUrlPath=$BASE_URL_PATH` (defaulting to the app slug). Verified
locally: with the prefix in place the page, static assets, fonts and the Streamlit
**websocket** all resolve; without it they 404. If a future AdaLab version strips the prefix
instead, set `BASE_URL_PATH=""` and flip `stripped_prefix` back to `true`.

### Persistent storage (deployment prerequisite)

Everything is stored under `DATA_DIR` (default `/asv-mnt/lab-data`), in a per-app
subdirectory: SQLite (`results.sqlite`) is the system of record and a long-format
`results.csv` mirror is rewritten on every submission. The app **refuses to start if that
path is not a mounted, writable volume** — student data is never written to disposable
container-local storage.

- This is a **single-replica** app: SQLite in WAL mode is a single-instance embedded
  database. Do **not** scale it horizontally.
- Persistence uses an **AdaLab Shared Volume (ASV)** with **Fast Mount ON** — required, not an
  optimization: SQLite over a plain network mount is the wrong shape for write traffic.
- One ASV is mounted into every course app, so a KUID's history across apps and years stays
  reachable; each app owns its own subdirectory.

One-time ASV setup runbook (a fresh volume has no filesystem permissions, so writes fail until
you chmod it — the mount can show green and every write still fails):

1. Create the volume on the AdaLab **Volumes** page.
2. Set its **ACL** (View / Mount / Edit) so you may attach it.
3. Mount it in a **lab**, open a terminal, and run:

   ```bash
   sudo chown root:$NB_GROUP <Volume_Name>
   sudo chmod 775 <Volume_Name>
   ```

4. In the **App Deployment** wizard, mount the volume at mount path `lab-data` (the platform
   prefixes `/asv-mnt/`) with **Fast Mount ON**.

Both layers are required: correct ACL without the chmod means writes fail; correct chmod
without ACL means you cannot mount. (Expected to become implicit in AdaLab v1.6.)

### Deployment order

Deploy in the order **Test → Build → Deploy**. Skipping Build breaks a first-time Deploy.

```yaml
asset: app
owner: {{ contact_email }}
version: 0.1.0
use: https://<your-adalab-host>/apps/{{ project_slug }}/
source: <repo URL>
data: $DATA_DIR/{{ project_slug }}/results.sqlite (mirror: results.csv, exports in-app)
```
